#h1{lit: a literate program that tangles itself}

This document is the source of `lit`, the one program that reads, writes and maintains documents
like it. It is a literate program in brashtag notation, the notation it implements: tangling it
produces the Go sources, the skill that teaches an assistant to use them, the tests and the
landing page; weaving it produces the document you are reading. A program that tangles itself
needs a place to start, and that is the bootstrap described in the chapter on the build. The
document is long, so it is split over files, one chapter for each tool or subsystem, with this
file as the root that introduces them.

The design follows from one decision about what a literate source is. A tangled file is a
function of the document, so the document is the source of truth, but people edit generated
files all the time. The tools therefore treat the pair (document, generated files) as one thing
that can be edited at either end. Everything after tangle exists to keep that honest: a
sourcemap, so that a place in one is a place in the other; absorb, which folds an edit of a
generated file back into the document; and a three-way merge, which reconciles edits made on
both sides.

Two consequences shape how the tools are meant to be used, and they are the reason the toolkit
exists in this form. First, the only thing that has to be agreed upon is the tangled output. The
narrative is a matter of taste, and with an assistant to write it, anybody can make a literate
fork of an existing codebase, organised the way they like to read it, without asking the project
to change anything. Making an existing codebase literate is therefore a job for the assistant,
and the document is a personal view of the code. Second, not everybody wants to work literate,
and nobody has to: a teammate keeps editing the generated files in their own editor, and absorb
carries those edits into the fork. The fork follows the project; the project does not follow the
fork.

A third idea closes the loop: the author can leave a gap in a chunk, marked
`#agent-fill-in{...}` with a sentence on what the code must do, and the toolkit gathers the
context of the gap from the whole document, asks an assistant for the code, checks it, writes it
in and records it for review. The ideas come from the literature on literate programming
#cite{knuth1984lp}, in particular its minimal, language-independent descendants
#cite{ramsey1994lp}, from the diff and merge literature #cite{myers1986diff}
#cite{khanna2007diff3}, and from the reason a CRDT was considered and rejected for the
synchronisation #cite{shapiro2011crdt}.

The program is a single binary written in Go, using only the standard library and the brashtag
parser, which is compiled in. There is nothing to install but the binary, and nothing that has
to be running on the machine but the operating system. The skill that teaches an assistant the
notation carries the same tools a second time, as Python scripts that use only Python's standard
library, because an assistant finds Python already present and a binary it would have to fetch;
the Go program is for people, the scripts are for assistants, and the build checks that they
agree.

This file is the root. Each chapter is a file of its own, listed here in the order in which it
is best read: the ideas and the tools first, the plain details in an appendix at the end. A tool
that is given this file reads them all as one text.

